{"type":"message","id":"41d0000c","parentId":"40e4d713","timestamp":"2026-05-27T12:32:17.905Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The API confirms the block is written successfully. The issue is probably that the block is being added as a child of the document root, but the document root might have a block_id that's different from the doc_token. Let me check the document structure to understand how blocks are organized in Feishu.\n\nActually, wait - I think I see the issue now. In the previous attempts, when I used `doc_token` as the parent block ID, blocks were successfully added. But the display might be cached or there might be a rendering issue.\n\nHowever, looking at the screenshot, it shows the document IS the right document (title \"Talus 知识库\" matches what I set), but with \"嘟吨 1731\" watermark and empty body. \n\nA refresh should clear the cache, though I can't ask the user to do that. But if API calls are succeeding yet content isn't displaying, the problem likely stems from how I'm structuring the block data itself - the block_type needs to be correct for text blocks to render properly.\n\nThe issue might be that text blocks aren't showing because there's no document body block to contain them. I'm wondering if I need to create a proper block hierarchy with a document body block at the root that contains the text blocks as children.\n\nLet me try a different approach - using the PATCH endpoint to update the document properties directly or checking the actual document structure by fetching the blocks again. I've confirmed the block structure is correct with block_type=2 and text elements, so the blocks should render fine. Since I can't ask the user to refresh, let me try creating a fresh document without going through the wiki API and see if that displays properly—if the original doc works but the wiki one doesn't, the issue might be with how wiki documents are being created or rendered. The blocks might have been added under the correct parent after all, so I should try querying the document structure directly to verify. If that doesn't work, I'm considering switching to markdown format instead, which Feishu supports natively and might render more reliably than my current approach.","thinkingSignature":"2ef79e7ebecb7b455c56143436ae280faf8e42175efe3b8b77948a32ff37e99c"},{"type":"toolCall","id":"call_function_2zjpy53by4gq_1","name":"exec","arguments":{"command":"python3 -c \"\nimport urllib.request, json\nwith open('/home/node/.openclaw/openclaw.json') as f: config = json.load(f)\nfeishu = config['channels']['feishu']['accounts']['main']\nreq = urllib.request.Request('https://open.feishu.cn/open-apis/auth/v3/tenant_access_token/internal',\n    data=json.dumps({'app_id': feishu['appId'], 'app_secret': feishu['appSecret']}).encode(),\n    headers={'Content-Type': 'application/json'})\ntoken = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(req).read())['tenant_access_token']\n\n# Create doc without wiki first\nreq2 = urllib.request.Request('https://open.feishu.cn/open-apis/docx/v1/documents',\n    data=json.dumps({'title': 'Talus Simple Doc'}).encode(),\n    headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {token}', 'Content-Type': 'application/json'})\nnew_doc = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(req2).read())['data']['document']['document_id']\n\n# Check what root block looks like\nreq3 = urllib.request.Request(\n    f'https://open.feishu.cn/open-apis/docx/v1/documents/{new_doc}/blocks?page_size=10',\n    headers={'Authorization': f'Bearer {token}'})\nroot_blocks = json.loads(urllib.request.urlopen(req3).read())\nprint('root blocks:')\nfor item in root_blocks.get('data', {}).get('items', []):\n    print(f'  id={item.get(\\\"block_id\\\")} type={item.get(\\\"block_type\\\")} parent={item.get(\\\"parent_id\\\")}')\n\""}}],"api":"anthropic-messages","provider":"minimax-portal","model":"MiniMax-M2.7","usage":{"input":711,"output":795,"cacheRead":54592,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":56098,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0